// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// TLS 1.3 (RFC 9846, which obsoletes RFC 8446): the full handshake of
// Figure 1 with every optional message present, followed by the
// post-handshake messages of §4.7. The server authenticates with a
// certificate, the server requests one from the client and the client
// authenticates with it, both certificates are issued by a certificate
// authority, and once the handshake completes the server hands out two
// resumption tickets and rotates its sending key. The companion model
// tls13-0rtt.vp is the resumption handshake those tickets are for.
//
// What is here, section by section:
//   §4.1   the running transcript hash, extended message by message;
//   §4.2   ClientHello and ServerHello as (random, key share) pairs;
//   §4.3.8 fresh key shares per connection (`generates ce`, `generates se`);
//   §4.4   EncryptedExtensions and CertificateRequest, carried as public
//          constants both sides fold into the transcript;
//   §4.5.1 Certificate as (name, key, CA signature over both), for both
//          endpoints, validated against a trust anchor received guarded;
//   §4.5.2 CertificateVerify over the transcript, prefixed by the context
//          string that separates a server's signature from a client's;
//   §4.5.3 Finished as a MAC keyed by a finished key expanded from the
//          sender's handshake traffic secret, over the transcript through
//          CertificateVerify;
//   §5.2   records as AEAD under the traffic secret of the sender, the
//   §5.3   third argument standing in for the record header and the
//          per-record nonce derived from the sequence number, so a record
//          is accepted only in its place (F.2, order protection);
//   §7.1   the key schedule from HKDF-Extract(0, 0) through the derived
//          steps to the main secret, every Derive-Secret carrying its own
//          label and the transcript hash of that point, plus the exporter
//          and resumption secrets hanging off the main secret;
//   §4.7.1 NewSessionTicket, with the PSK derived from the resumption
//          secret and the ticket nonce, two tickets on one connection;
//   §4.7.3 KeyUpdate under the old key, then §7.2's next-generation
//   §7.2   application traffic secret for the record that follows.
//
// Expected: c1c0c0c0c0c0e0c0c0c1c0c1a0a0a0a0a0f0f0, at two sessions and
// at one. That code was derived by reading the protocol, not by running
// the model; confirm it before pinning it.
//
// The queries follow Appendix F, and three of them fail on purpose. Read
// each failure with the RFC open, because each one is a sentence the RFC
// itself wrote.
//
// Secrecy of the session keys (F.1), and CK01's proviso:
//   confidentiality? sHsTraffic                               FAILS
//   confidentiality? sHsTraffic  [precondition: server completes]  HOLDS
//   confidentiality? cHsTraffic_c [precondition: client completes] HOLDS
//   confidentiality? sApTraffic  [precondition: server completes]  HOLDS
//   confidentiality? cApTraffic_c [precondition: client completes] HOLDS
//   confidentiality? exporterSecret [precondition: server completes] HOLDS
//   equivalence? sApTraffic, sApTraffic_c [both complete]        HOLDS
// The server derives its handshake secrets from whatever key share
// arrives in the ClientHello, before anything has been checked, so an
// attacker that opens a connection with a share of its own owns a
// sHsTraffic. F.1 says as much: "the attacker can establish its own
// session keys with the server, but those session keys are distinct from
// those established by the client." What the server does with such a key
// is halt, at the client's CertificateVerify, without ever sending
// application data under it. The gated queries say exactly what F.1 says
// under "Establishing the same session keys": the guarantee holds
// "provided that it completes successfully on each endpoint". The
// precondition restricts each query to executions in which the named
// endpoint goes on past its last check, and in every such execution the
// keys are a two-party secret and the two sides hold the same one. The
// client has the same shape on its side: it derives cHsTraffic_c before
// it can decrypt the server's flight, so the ungated client-side query
// would fail the same way, and the gated one holds.
//
// Application data (F.2 confidentiality, F.1 forward secrecy):
//   confidentiality? appMsg1     HOLDS
//   confidentiality? appMsg2     HOLDS
//   confidentiality? appMsg3     FAILS
// Phase 1 leaks both long-term signing keys after the connection is over.
// Nothing recorded opens: the traffic secrets descend from the ephemeral
// dhe = DH_KEX(gce, se), and neither signing key is an ingredient. That is
// F.1's "forward secret with respect to long-term keys". Phase 2 leaks
// sApTraffic1, the server's sending secret after its KeyUpdate. appMsg3
// was encrypted under it and falls; appMsg2 was encrypted under the
// generation before, which HKDF-Expand-Label cannot be run backwards to,
// and holds. That is F.2's "forward secrecy after key change", and the
// only failure among the application data. F.2 also says the other
// direction is not provided: leak generation 0 instead and every later
// generation follows from it. This model does not show that, because one
// file can leak in one order.
//
// Resumption (F.1, tickets):
//   confidentiality? resPsk2     HOLDS
// Phase 2 also leaks resPsk1, the PSK behind the first ticket. The second
// ticket's PSK is a separate expansion of the resumption secret under its
// own nonce, and survives, as does the resumption secret itself under the
// traffic-secret leak: "the resumption secret computed by connection N
// and needed to form connection N+1 is separate from the traffic keys
// used by connection N".
//
// Endpoint identities (F.1):
//   confidentiality? clientId    FAILS
// The client's identity travels only inside its Certificate, sealed under
// cHsTraffic_c after the server has authenticated, so a network attacker
// never sees it. It is disclosed all the same, and the reason is the
// second scenario: there the client is configured to reach Mallory, whose
// signing key the attacker holds, and the attacker completes the server
// side of that handshake with Mallory's genuine certificate. The client
// then does what F.1 promises it will do: "it only reveals its identity
// to an authenticated server". Mallory is one. Verifpal's confidentiality
// query asks whether the attacker knows the value, not whom the client
// meant to tell, and a `knows private` identity is the same value in
// every run. Delete the second scenario and this query is expected to
// hold. The server's identity is public here, as a hostname is; the RFC
// protects the server's certificate only against passive attackers, and
// an active one learns it by opening a connection.
//
// Peer authentication (F.1) and integrity with non-replay (F.2):
//   authentication? Server -> Client: serverFlight   HOLDS
//   authentication? Client -> Server: clientFlight   HOLDS
//   authentication? Client -> Server: appData1       HOLDS
//   authentication? Server -> Client: appData2       HOLDS
//   authentication? Server -> Client: appData3       HOLDS
// Verifpal's authentication query is injective agreement, so a hold rules
// out replay as well as forgery. The server's flight is accepted only
// under a signature, by a key a trust anchor vouches for, over a
// transcript that contains the client's own key share and random; the
// client's flight is accepted under the same construction, over a
// transcript that also contains the server's certificate and Finished.
// The second half is where the scenarios[] block earns its place. The
// client that talks to Mallory signs a CertificateVerify for that
// handshake, and the attacker holds it afterwards. Reusing it against the
// honest server is the attack F.1 describes for constructions "that do
// not bind to the server's certificate" ([PSK-FINISHED], [Kraw16]), and
// it fails here because the client's signature covers the server's
// Certificate and the shares of this connection. Every record after the
// handshake is sealed under a secret that mixes both ephemeral shares, so
// a record lifted from a concurrent connection decrypts nowhere, and the
// sequence number in the additional data keeps the four records the
// server sends under one key from standing in for each other.
//
// Uniqueness of the session keys (F.1):
//   freshness? sApTraffic, freshness? cApTraffic_c   HOLD
// Both traffic secrets descend from the per-connection se and ce; §7 says
// the transcript's randoms alone would do this, and they are in the
// transcript too.
//
// Modelling notes.
//
// The scenarios[] block gives the client two configurations: the
// certificate of the honest server, or Mallory's. In TLS that
// configuration is a hostname. It is a certificate here because Verifpal
// tells a corrupt peer from an honest one by following a leaked key into
// the values computed from it, and a bare name is computed from nothing:
// Mallory's certificate carries Mallory's public key, whose private half
// leaks, so a run configured with it is a run with a corrupt peer and its
// claims are not the protocol's to answer for. The client reads the
// expected name out of the configured certificate and compares it with
// the name in the Certificate message it receives, which is hostname
// verification as a browser does it; nothing else about the configured
// certificate is consulted. Drop that comparison and the attacker presents
// Mallory's genuine certificate to a client that wanted the honest server,
// which is the misbinding of F.9.
//
// Client certificates change the reading of this model against the
// server-only handshake that browsers run. Without them the server has no
// way to tell the client from an attacker with a key share, and every
// claim the server makes about who it talked to fails: authentication of
// the client's flight and data, and the confidentiality of the server's
// own session secrets, since "the client" may simply be the attacker.
// With them those claims hold, and the ungated sHsTraffic query above is
// what remains of that story.
//
// Not modelled: parameter negotiation and its downgrade protection
// (there is one cipher suite and one group), HelloRetryRequest and
// cookies, the PSK and 0-RTT modes (tls13-0rtt.vp), post-handshake
// client authentication, the exporter interface over the exporter secret,
// alerts, record padding, and the within-connection sequence-number
// checks beyond what the additional data expresses. Key compromise
// impersonation (F.1) is not tested either: it needs a handshake run
// after the client's key has leaked, and every handshake here runs at
// phase 0.

attacker[active]

principal CA[
	knows private caKey
	caPk = PUBKEY(caKey)
]

principal Server[
	knows private sigKey
	knows public serverName
	gSigKey = PUBKEY(sigKey)
]

principal Client[
	knows private clientSigKey
	knows private clientId
	clientPk = PUBKEY(clientSigKey)
]

// A second certified server. Its signing key is the attacker's from the
// start, so it stands for any legitimately certified site the attacker
// controls.
principal Mallory[
	knows private mkey
	knows public mallName
	gmSigKey = PUBKEY(mkey)
	leaks mkey
]

// Enrolment with the CA is out of band and guarded: a CA that certifies
// whatever key arrives on an open wire would be an oracle.
Server -> CA: [gSigKey]
Client -> CA: [clientPk]
Mallory -> CA: [gmSigKey]

principal CA[
	knows public serverName, mallName
	knows private clientId
	// §4.5.1: a certificate binds an identity to a key. The client's
	// identity is private, since the client's certificate must stay
	// confidential (F.1), so its certificate reaches it as the CA's
	// signature alone and the client assembles the message itself.
	serverCertSig = SIGN(caKey, CONCAT(serverName, gSigKey))
	clientCertSig = SIGN(caKey, CONCAT(clientId, clientPk))
	mallCertSig = SIGN(caKey, CONCAT(mallName, gmSigKey))
]

// The trust anchor is distributed independently (§4.5.1), hence guarded.
CA -> Server: [caPk], [serverCertSig]
CA -> Client: [caPk], [clientCertSig]
CA -> Mallory: [mallCertSig]

principal Server[
	serverCert = CONCAT(serverName, gSigKey, serverCertSig)
]

principal Mallory[
	mallCert = CONCAT(mallName, gmSigKey, mallCertSig)
]

// The client's configuration: which site it means to reach. See the
// modelling notes above for why this is a certificate and not a name.
Server -> Client: [serverCert]
Mallory -> Client: [mallCert]

principal Client[
	knows public peerCert
	knows public encryptedExtensions, certificateRequest, updateNotRequested
	knows public seq0, seq1, seq2, seq3
	knows public labelDerived, labelFinished
	knows public labelCHsTraffic, labelSHsTraffic
	knows public labelCApTraffic, labelSApTraffic
	knows public labelExpMaster, labelResMaster
	knows public labelResumption, labelTrafficUpd
	knows public ctxServerCV, ctxClientCV
	expectedName, _, _ = SPLIT(peerCert)
	// ClientHello (§4.2.2): random and key share, fresh per connection.
	generates cr, ce
	gce = PUBKEY(ce)
]

Client -> Server: cr, gce

principal Server[
	knows public encryptedExtensions, certificateRequest, updateNotRequested
	knows public seq0, seq1, seq2, seq3
	knows public labelDerived, labelFinished
	knows public labelCHsTraffic, labelSHsTraffic
	knows public labelCApTraffic, labelSApTraffic
	knows public labelExpMaster, labelResMaster
	knows public labelResumption, labelTrafficUpd
	knows public ctxServerCV, ctxClientCV
	// ServerHello (§4.2.3).
	generates sr, se
	gse = PUBKEY(se)
	dhe = DH_KEX(gce, se)
	// §7.1. HKDF(salt, ikm, nil) is HKDF-Extract; HKDF(secret, label,
	// transcript) is Derive-Secret. With no PSK the early secret is
	// HKDF-Extract(0, 0), a public value, and secrecy enters with dhe.
	earlySecret = HKDF(nil, nil, nil)
	derivedES = HKDF(earlySecret, labelDerived, nil)
	handshakeSecret = HKDF(derivedES, dhe, nil)
	transcriptSH = HASH(cr, gce, sr, gse)
	cHsTraffic = HKDF(handshakeSecret, labelCHsTraffic, transcriptSH)
	sHsTraffic = HKDF(handshakeSecret, labelSHsTraffic, transcriptSH)
	derivedHS = HKDF(handshakeSecret, labelDerived, nil)
	mainSecret = HKDF(derivedHS, nil, nil)
	// §4.4: EncryptedExtensions and CertificateRequest extend the
	// transcript; §4.5.1: then the Certificate.
	transcriptCR = HASH(transcriptSH, encryptedExtensions, certificateRequest)
	transcriptCert = HASH(transcriptCR, serverName, gSigKey, serverCertSig)
	// §4.5.2: CertificateVerify, with the server context string.
	serverCV = SIGN(sigKey, CONCAT(ctxServerCV, transcriptCert))
	transcriptCV = HASH(transcriptCert, serverCV)
	// §4.5.3: Finished under the finished key of the base key.
	sFinishedKey = HKDF(sHsTraffic, labelFinished, nil)
	serverFinished = MAC(sFinishedKey, transcriptCV)
	// Certificate, CertificateVerify and Finished, sealed under the
	// server handshake traffic secret (§5.2), record 0 under that key.
	serverFlight = AEAD_ENC(sHsTraffic, CONCAT(serverName, gSigKey, serverCertSig, serverCV, serverFinished), seq0)
	transcriptSFin = HASH(transcriptCV, serverFinished)
	cApTraffic = HKDF(mainSecret, labelCApTraffic, transcriptSFin)
	sApTraffic = HKDF(mainSecret, labelSApTraffic, transcriptSFin)
	exporterSecret = HKDF(mainSecret, labelExpMaster, transcriptSFin)
]

Server -> Client: sr, gse, serverFlight

principal Client[
	dhe_c = DH_KEX(gse, ce)
	earlySecret_c = HKDF(nil, nil, nil)
	derivedES_c = HKDF(earlySecret_c, labelDerived, nil)
	handshakeSecret_c = HKDF(derivedES_c, dhe_c, nil)
	transcriptSH_c = HASH(cr, gce, sr, gse)
	cHsTraffic_c = HKDF(handshakeSecret_c, labelCHsTraffic, transcriptSH_c)
	sHsTraffic_c = HKDF(handshakeSecret_c, labelSHsTraffic, transcriptSH_c)
	derivedHS_c = HKDF(handshakeSecret_c, labelDerived, nil)
	mainSecret_c = HKDF(derivedHS_c, nil, nil)
	serverFlightDec = AEAD_DEC(sHsTraffic_c, serverFlight, seq0)?
	certName, certKey, certSig, serverCV_c, serverFinished_c = SPLIT(serverFlightDec)
	// §4.5.1.3: the chain is validated against the trust anchor, and the
	// certified name is the one the client meant to reach.
	_ = SIGNVERIF(caPk, CONCAT(certName, certKey), certSig)?
	_ = ASSERT(certName, expectedName)?
	transcriptCR_c = HASH(transcriptSH_c, encryptedExtensions, certificateRequest)
	transcriptCert_c = HASH(transcriptCR_c, certName, certKey, certSig)
	// §4.5.2: the signature is checked under the key in the certificate,
	// not under anything the client knew in advance.
	_ = SIGNVERIF(certKey, CONCAT(ctxServerCV, transcriptCert_c), serverCV_c)?
	transcriptCV_c = HASH(transcriptCert_c, serverCV_c)
	sFinishedKey_c = HKDF(sHsTraffic_c, labelFinished, nil)
	expectedServerFinished = MAC(sFinishedKey_c, transcriptCV_c)
	_ = ASSERT(serverFinished_c, expectedServerFinished)?
	transcriptSFin_c = HASH(transcriptCV_c, serverFinished_c)
	cApTraffic_c = HKDF(mainSecret_c, labelCApTraffic, transcriptSFin_c)
	sApTraffic_c = HKDF(mainSecret_c, labelSApTraffic, transcriptSFin_c)
	exporterSecret_c = HKDF(mainSecret_c, labelExpMaster, transcriptSFin_c)
	// The client's authentication block (§4.5, Table 2): Certificate,
	// CertificateVerify with the client context string over the
	// transcript through server Finished, and Finished under the client
	// handshake traffic secret.
	transcriptCCert = HASH(transcriptSFin_c, clientId, clientPk, clientCertSig)
	clientCV = SIGN(clientSigKey, CONCAT(ctxClientCV, transcriptCCert))
	transcriptCCV = HASH(transcriptCCert, clientCV)
	cFinishedKey_c = HKDF(cHsTraffic_c, labelFinished, nil)
	clientFinished = MAC(cFinishedKey_c, transcriptCCV)
	clientFlight = AEAD_ENC(cHsTraffic_c, CONCAT(clientId, clientPk, clientCertSig, clientCV, clientFinished), seq0)
	// §7.1: the resumption secret covers the transcript through client
	// Finished.
	transcriptCFin_c = HASH(transcriptCCV, clientFinished)
	resumptionSecret_c = HKDF(mainSecret_c, labelResMaster, transcriptCFin_c)
	generates appMsg1
	appData1 = AEAD_ENC(cApTraffic_c, appMsg1, seq0)
]

Client -> Server: clientFlight, appData1

principal Server[
	clientFlightDec = AEAD_DEC(cHsTraffic, clientFlight, seq0)?
	clientId_s, clientPk_s, clientCertSig_s, clientCV_s, clientFinished_s = SPLIT(clientFlightDec)
	_ = SIGNVERIF(caPk, CONCAT(clientId_s, clientPk_s), clientCertSig_s)?
	transcriptCCert_s = HASH(transcriptSFin, clientId_s, clientPk_s, clientCertSig_s)
	_ = SIGNVERIF(clientPk_s, CONCAT(ctxClientCV, transcriptCCert_s), clientCV_s)?
	transcriptCCV_s = HASH(transcriptCCert_s, clientCV_s)
	cFinishedKey_s = HKDF(cHsTraffic, labelFinished, nil)
	expectedClientFinished = MAC(cFinishedKey_s, transcriptCCV_s)
	_ = ASSERT(clientFinished_s, expectedClientFinished)?
	transcriptCFin = HASH(transcriptCCV_s, clientFinished_s)
	resumptionSecret = HKDF(mainSecret, labelResMaster, transcriptCFin)
	// §4.5.3: application data from the client is read only once its
	// Finished has been verified.
	appMsg1_s = AEAD_DEC(cApTraffic, appData1, seq0)?
	// §4.7.1: two NewSessionTickets, each with its own nonce and so its
	// own PSK; the tickets are records 0 and 1 under sApTraffic.
	generates ticketNonce1, ticketNonce2
	resPsk1 = HKDF(resumptionSecret, labelResumption, ticketNonce1)
	resPsk2 = HKDF(resumptionSecret, labelResumption, ticketNonce2)
	newSessionTicket1 = AEAD_ENC(sApTraffic, ticketNonce1, seq0)
	newSessionTicket2 = AEAD_ENC(sApTraffic, ticketNonce2, seq1)
	generates appMsg2
	appData2 = AEAD_ENC(sApTraffic, appMsg2, seq2)
	// §4.7.3: KeyUpdate goes out under the old key; §7.2: the next
	// generation is one HKDF-Expand-Label away, and its sequence numbers
	// start over (§5.3).
	keyUpdate = AEAD_ENC(sApTraffic, updateNotRequested, seq3)
	sApTraffic1 = HKDF(sApTraffic, labelTrafficUpd, nil)
	generates appMsg3
	appData3 = AEAD_ENC(sApTraffic1, appMsg3, seq0)
]

Server -> Client: newSessionTicket1, newSessionTicket2, appData2, keyUpdate, appData3

principal Client[
	ticketNonce1_c = AEAD_DEC(sApTraffic_c, newSessionTicket1, seq0)?
	ticketNonce2_c = AEAD_DEC(sApTraffic_c, newSessionTicket2, seq1)?
	resPsk1_c = HKDF(resumptionSecret_c, labelResumption, ticketNonce1_c)
	resPsk2_c = HKDF(resumptionSecret_c, labelResumption, ticketNonce2_c)
	appMsg2_c = AEAD_DEC(sApTraffic_c, appData2, seq2)?
	keyUpdate_c = AEAD_DEC(sApTraffic_c, keyUpdate, seq3)?
	_ = ASSERT(keyUpdate_c, updateNotRequested)?
	sApTraffic1_c = HKDF(sApTraffic_c, labelTrafficUpd, nil)
	appMsg3_c = AEAD_DEC(sApTraffic1_c, appData3, seq0)?
]

phase[1]

// Both long-term keys are compromised after the connection has closed
// (F.1, forward secrecy with respect to long-term keys).
principal Server[
	leaks sigKey
]

principal Client[
	leaks clientSigKey
]

phase[2]

// Then the server's current sending secret and one ticket's PSK (F.2,
// forward secrecy after key change; F.1, independence of tickets).
principal Server[
	leaks sApTraffic1, resPsk1
]

scenarios[
	Client[peerCert = serverCert]
	Client[peerCert = mallCert]
]

queries[
	confidentiality? sHsTraffic
	confidentiality? sHsTraffic[
		precondition[Server -> Client: appData2]
	]
	confidentiality? cHsTraffic_c[
		precondition[Client -> Server: clientFlight]
	]
	confidentiality? sApTraffic[
		precondition[Server -> Client: appData2]
	]
	confidentiality? cApTraffic_c[
		precondition[Client -> Server: clientFlight]
	]
	confidentiality? exporterSecret[
		precondition[Server -> Client: appData2]
	]
	equivalence? sApTraffic, sApTraffic_c[
		precondition[Server -> Client: appData2]
		precondition[Client -> Server: clientFlight]
	]
	confidentiality? appMsg1
	confidentiality? appMsg2
	confidentiality? appMsg3
	confidentiality? resPsk2
	confidentiality? clientId
	authentication? Server -> Client: serverFlight
	authentication? Client -> Server: clientFlight
	authentication? Client -> Server: appData1
	authentication? Server -> Client: appData2
	authentication? Server -> Client: appData3
	freshness? sApTraffic
	freshness? cApTraffic_c
]
